INT. PLANETARIUM DOME, CITY SCIENCE MUSEUM - NIGHT

A hemisphere of dark. Rows of reclined seats in concentric rings. At the center, the projector: a black iron dumbbell on a pedestal, gleaming. Stars drift overhead, paused at midsummer. The only other light is the emergency green over the exit.

TOMASZ KRÓL, fifties, in a security guard's jacket, a name tag reading T. KROL, stands at the dome's rim with a flashlight he isn't using.

The projector's access hatch is open. Inside, a woman's legs. Then the rest of her: ADAORA NWOSU, forties, a technician's harness, clipboards of schematics. She is hanging from the dome's catwalk by two cables, laying a case onto a cart.

DESHAWN "DEZ" HARTIGAN, thirties, in a docent's blazer, watches from the front row with his arms folded. LUCINDA VASQUEZ-WILDE, sixties, silver bob, pearl earrings, stands at the aisle, one hand resting on the back of a seat. She looks like she owns the dome. Perhaps she does.

TOMASZ
Time check.

DEZ
Eleven fifty-two. Rounds at twelve fifteen.

ADAORA
(from the catwalk)
I need the lens case.

LUCINDA
It's by your left hand.

ADAORA
My left's holding a cable.

LUCINDA
Then use the right.

Adaora does. She slides the case across and catches it. Her wrist wobbles. Dez rises half an inch from the seat, then sits again.

DEZ
(to himself)
Easy.

TOMASZ
You said the lens was in the base.

ADAORA
It was in the base.

TOMASZ
It's in the housing.

ADAORA
Then it moved.

TOMASZ
Lenses don't move.

LUCINDA
This one does.

Tomasz turns the flashlight on at last. He sweeps it across the faces in the dark, one by one. They don't blink.

TOMASZ
How many people know it moved?

DEZ
Us.

TOMASZ
And the man who moved it.

LUCINDA
Whom you've never met.

TOMASZ
(quietly)
Yes I have.

Silence. The stars hang overhead, patient as an audience.

ADAORA
(from above)
Tomasz.

TOMASZ
Not now.

ADAORA
Tomasz --

TOMASZ
I said not now.

He lowers the light. Dez picks at a thread on his sleeve. Lucinda doesn't move.

TOMASZ (CONT'D)
(to the room)
Whatever happens at twelve fifteen, I don't want to hear a word of it after.

DEZ
Understood.

LUCINDA
Naturally.

ADAORA
Understood.

He nods. He crosses the aisle. He looks up at the projector, at the delicate, oiled, indifferent machine, and puts his hand flat against its cold side as if feeling for a pulse.

TOMASZ
(softly)
Thirty years.

Dez stands. He walks to the aisle and stops beside Lucinda. She does not look at him.

DEZ
He's going to see it, you know. The last frame.

LUCINDA
He already has.

Adaora lowers herself from the catwalk onto the stage, her boots clicking on the floor. She holds the lens case in both hands, the way you'd hold a sleeping child.

ADAORA
It's good. It's the real one.

LUCINDA
Of course it is.

Dez glances at his watch.

DEZ
Eleven fifty-nine.

Tomasz turns off the flashlight. The dome goes dark except for the green glow at the exit and the slow-turning stars.

TOMASZ
(very low)
Let's go.

They move. Not in a group, but each one separately, in a loose line along the aisle toward the exit, their footsteps muffled by the carpet. Lucinda is last. At the door, she stops.

LUCINDA
(to the empty dome)
Goodnight, old friend.

She waits. The stars shift an inch as if the dome were breathing. Then she goes.

The projector sits alone on its pedestal, a dark dumbbell under a dead sky.

From the corridor, faintly, a key turns in a lock.
